# Sourced by /usr/bin/start-sway, with `set -o allexport`, immediately before it
# execs sway. Fedora's wrapper offers this file for environment variables. What
# it is load-bearing for here is ordering: it is the last user-owned hook that
# runs before the compositor reads a single line of config.
#
# What runs is the linker. scripts/link-sway creates the seventeen symlinks the
# config points at, and the config names them without ever checking: the bar's
# status_command is ~/.config/sway/statusbar, the lock timeout is
# ~/.config/sway/idle, and $mod+d, $mod+Shift+d and $mod+Shift+i are display,
# present and live. A link that was never created doesn't announce itself. It
# fails at the moment sway tries to use it, which for a keybinding is whenever
# someone next presses the key. Five had drifted this way before a dead bar
# made it obvious, all five added by a single commit that nobody reran the linker
# after.
#
# Here rather than only an exec_always in config.d, for two reasons. config.d
# is itself one of the links, so a config.d that went missing takes every
# exec_always in it down with it and there is nothing left to do the repair.
# And swaybar gives a missing status_command a few attempts and then stops, so
# a link that appears once sway is already parsing is a link that arrives too
# late for the bar. 40-autostart.conf runs it again anyway, to catch a pull
# that lands mid-session.
#
# Nothing is exported from here, so allexport has nothing to catch. Output goes
# to the journal under the link-sway tag rather than into the session log,
# where `journalctl --user -t link-sway` can find it; in steady state the
# linker prints nothing, so anything there at all is a link that moved.
#
# A failure is deliberately not fatal. start-sway runs without `set -e` and
# ignores what this file returns, which is the behaviour we want: a stale
# symlink costs one binding, and a session that refuses to start costs the
# whole login.
"${SYSBASE_HOME:-$HOME/sys_base}/scripts/link-sway" 2>&1 |
  systemd-cat -t link-sway -p notice
